1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,880
Hey everyone, we're Team VectroSync, and welcome to our live prototype demonstration.

2
00:00:06,880 --> 00:00:13,040
We built this cyber-physical digital twin specifically for Oil India Limited's Well #14...

3
00:00:13,040 --> 00:00:17,200
...located in the Baghewala heavy oil field out in Rajasthan.

4
00:00:17,200 --> 00:00:22,000
Now, heavy oil thermal extraction is brutal on equipment.

5
00:00:22,000 --> 00:00:26,480
Operators pump high-pressure steam downhole to liquefy the crude,

6
00:00:26,480 --> 00:00:30,080
but as the reservoir inevitably cools down,

7
00:00:30,080 --> 00:00:36,320
that crude turns thick as tar—shooting past 11,000 centipoise.

8
00:00:36,320 --> 00:00:39,440
When the sucker rod pump strokes downward,

9
00:00:39,440 --> 00:00:44,880
that heavy viscous fluid creates massive annular Couette shear drag.

10
00:00:44,880 --> 00:00:49,600
The fluid drag literally overpowers the weight of the rods. They float,

11
00:00:49,600 --> 00:00:53,760
they buckle under compression, and eventually they snap.

12
00:00:53,760 --> 00:00:57,920
Existing tools out there like ForeSite or Lufkin SAM,

13
00:00:57,920 --> 00:01:03,840
they're completely reactive, only alerting after the rod has already broken in half.

14
00:01:03,840 --> 00:01:09,520
And by then, you're looking at millions of rupees in workover rig repairs and lost oil production.

15
00:01:09,520 --> 00:01:12,080
That's the exact problem we set out to solve.

16
00:01:12,080 --> 00:01:17,760
VectroSync isn't just another monitoring dashboard—it's an autonomous edge controller.

17
00:01:17,760 --> 00:01:20,480
Look right up here at our mission control header:

18
00:01:20,480 --> 00:01:26,160
our controller is running live with an edge execution latency of just 0.35 milliseconds,

19
00:01:26,160 --> 00:01:30,800
communicating over industrial Modbus-TCP port 502.

20
00:01:30,800 --> 00:01:33,600
Right now, the well is running at normal baseline.

21
00:01:33,600 --> 00:01:37,200
The formation is hot at 218 degrees Celsius,

22
00:01:37,200 --> 00:01:40,240
viscosity is low at 75 centipoise,

23
00:01:40,240 --> 00:01:45,280
and our downhole tension is sitting safe at positive 8.4 kilonewtons.

24
00:01:45,280 --> 00:01:47,680
Look at our dynamometer cards on the right:

25
00:01:47,680 --> 00:01:52,000
both surface and downhole cards are tracking cleanly inside the green envelope.

26
00:01:52,000 --> 00:01:54,400
Let me take you under the hood for a second.

27
00:01:54,400 --> 00:02:00,720
If we click Tab 2, this is our spatiotemporal depth-stress map.

28
00:02:00,720 --> 00:02:06,640
It resolves the full continuous axial stress tensor across 144 nodes

29
00:02:06,640 --> 00:02:10,240
and a 360-degree crank cycle.

30
00:02:10,240 --> 00:02:14,320
And over in Tab 3, our predictive horizon couples

31
00:02:14,400 --> 00:02:18,720
Boberg-Lantz thermal dissipation with emulsion rheology,

32
00:02:18,720 --> 00:02:24,480
forecasting temperature drops and viscosity spikes hours before they actually hit the wellhead.

33
00:02:24,480 --> 00:02:27,600
Now, let's trigger a real failure.

34
00:02:27,600 --> 00:02:32,880
Watch what happens when I click Scenario A—the Baghewala Freeze.

35
00:02:32,880 --> 00:02:39,280
The reservoir cools to 66 degrees, and viscosity spikes to 11,800 centipoise.

36
00:02:39,280 --> 00:02:42,320
Look at the wellbore on the left turning red!

37
00:02:42,320 --> 00:02:46,000
On the downstroke, fluid drag completely grabs the rod string.

38
00:02:46,000 --> 00:02:51,840
Downhole tension just plunged into negative compression at minus 1.81 kilonewtons.

39
00:02:51,840 --> 00:02:53,760
The rod has buckled,

40
00:02:53,760 --> 00:02:57,360
and immediately, our supervisory system trips

41
00:02:57,360 --> 00:03:00,560
a Level 3 emergency stop to save the string.

42
00:03:00,560 --> 00:03:04,480
Now, let's see VectroSync fix this proactively.

43
00:03:04,480 --> 00:03:06,240
I'll click Scenario B.

44
00:03:06,240 --> 00:03:07,680
Watch the pumping speed:

45
00:03:07,680 --> 00:03:11,760
our Model Predictive Controller detects the cooling trend in advance,

46
00:03:11,760 --> 00:03:18,000
and automatically throttles surface speed from 5.2 down to 2.8 strokes per minute.

47
00:03:18,000 --> 00:03:21,520
Because fluid shear drag drops quadratically with speed,

48
00:03:21,520 --> 00:03:27,360
downhole tension instantly recovers back to positive 2.36 kilonewtons.

49
00:03:27,360 --> 00:03:31,440
That downhole card snaps right back into the green safe zone,

50
00:03:31,440 --> 00:03:35,040
keeping production flowing without any intervention or downtime.

51
00:03:35,040 --> 00:03:38,960
Finally, what happens if field communication drops?

52
00:03:39,040 --> 00:03:41,680
Let's sever Modbus in Scenario C.

53
00:03:41,680 --> 00:03:44,560
Look at port 502—communication lost.

54
00:03:44,560 --> 00:03:46,880
But the system doesn't freeze or crash.

55
00:03:46,880 --> 00:03:50,640
It instantly transitions into Level 2 Protective mode,

56
00:03:50,640 --> 00:03:57,200
executing an automated 3-stroke ramp-down to a safe 2.0 strokes per minute mechanical fallback.

57
00:03:57,200 --> 00:04:02,640
When you scale this across all 23 heavy-oil wells in the Baghewala basin,

58
00:04:03,360 --> 00:04:08,960
VectroSync saves an auditable 14.8 crore rupees every single year,

59
00:04:08,960 --> 00:04:15,040
eliminating 47 workover interventions and cutting 300 tonnes of wasted energy emissions.

60
00:04:15,040 --> 00:04:20,720
Real physics, autonomous edge control, and zero mock shortcuts.

61
00:04:20,720 --> 00:04:23,760
That is Team VectroSync.

62
00:04:23,760 --> 00:04:24,560
Thanks for watching.

